WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.250
(rustling)

00:00:04.619 --> 00:00:06.869
(drumming)

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:12.530
- Hi, my name's Garrett Parrish,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:14.010
and I'm a senior at MIT studying

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.320
mechanical engineering and
entertainment technology.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.610
I'm also a musician,
designer, an engineer,

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.030
and I spend my time combining

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.587
all 3 of those disciplines
using technology.

00:00:22.587 --> 00:00:24.837
(drumming)

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.920
I usually wake up at around
8 o'clock in the morning.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.370
Start, you know, getting
ready for the day,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:43.600
have my Trader Joe's raspberry
oatmeal every single day.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.320
Then I usually leave the
house about 9:30 or so.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.540
I usually don't have class in the morning,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.520
so I either work on some
of my music or my homework,

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:55.220
or some of the other
projects I'm working on.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.300
Grab some lunch in Kendall
Square or around campus.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.030
Go to class, usually in the
afternoon for a couple hours.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.880
And then at night I usually have rehearsal

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.440
or some other, you know, meeting

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.530
with some of the various activities I do,

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.530
and then come back to
the house for dinner,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.160
hang out with friends,
you know, do my work,

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.153
and then eventually get back to sleep.

00:01:16.153 --> 00:01:18.700
(relaxed music)

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.980
- You mentioned that combining music,

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:23.690
technology, engineering.

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:25.650
Sort of broadly speaking,
what does that look like?

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:26.860
What does that mean?

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.580
- So, I tried very, very consciously

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:30.973
not to pigeonhole myself
into one discipline.

00:01:30.973 --> 00:01:34.150
I think it's really important
for everybody to do that.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.010
Ever since I was really young

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.760
I've always done both
music and engineering,

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.060
and that was, you know, my
path throughout high school

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.820
and into college, because
I'm equally passionate

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.940
about both and, you know,
both are absolutely critical

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.890
to who I am as a human being.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:53.890
And I think that when you
can combine them effectively,

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.540
you can achieve far more than you could

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.310
with just one of them.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:01.040
So, it was really MIT that
showed me that that was possible,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.550
and that I could not have to keep

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:04.780
those two parts of my life separate,

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.680
and just do 'em both at the same time.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.960
- So, what is, what does your
living situation look like?

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.640
- So, I live in a co-ed
literary fraternity

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:18.930
of about 60 students
called the Number Six Club.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:22.030
We live on campus, even
though it's privately owned,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:25.500
and we eat together, socialize together,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.030
work together, hang out
together, go out together,

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.630
and it's a really, really
wonderful community of people.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.250
One of the most interesting parts about it

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.210
is that there's a high constituent

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:36.120
of international students.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.840
So, I'm actually one of only about

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.040
3 or 4 Americans that live in the house,

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.180
and it's really interesting to be able

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.970
to be exposed to different cultures

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:44.840
from all around the world.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:47.940
We have Greece, Sweden, Germany,

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.180
Columbia, Venezuela, all over the place.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.100
I mean, it's really cool,

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.840
because everyone's kind of an outsider,

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.570
so it really brings everybody together in,

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.890
like a true family of students.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.470
- Walk me around this room then.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.820
Lot going on here.
- Sure, so, this is,

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:06.060
I call this my castle,
'cause I really like castles,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.290
and because I'm obsessed
with Disney, obviously.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:10.070
- That's thematic.
- Yeah.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.360
So, first and most important
part is the ceiling.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.140
This is a replica of the night sky

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.770
my, the girl who had the room before me

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:21.270
actually painted it, but then
I installed all the lights.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:22.930
And one of the great
things about Number Six

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:24.950
is that they allow you
to renovate your room,

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.640
and do what you want
with it within reason,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.010
and even reimburse you for parts of it.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.160
So, I put in a floor, I
painted, added the lights,

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.247
and really, you know, made it my own space

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:38.770
and a place that I feel
comfortable coming home to.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.509
So, it's not just a dorm room.

00:03:40.509 --> 00:03:43.150
(relaxed music)

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.900
So, over here I kinda have my music space.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:48.700
So, this is my electronic drum set,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.130
which allows me to play drums
at any time of the night,

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.920
and not bother anybody.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.670
So, this is my desk where
I do most of my work.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.000
Right now, I'm working for an arrangement

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.050
for the jazz ensemble that I play in here.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:01.810
It's debuting in about a month,

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.750
and behind my desk I have
a lot of the drawings I do,

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.110
And I've always been fascinated
with the Disney characters,

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.380
and I really was interested

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:10.950
in getting to know more about visual art,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.990
and I actually took a
drawing class here at MIT

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:16.130
which really opened my
eyes about not being afraid

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.570
to just, you know, dive into something.

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:20.940
So, these are a lot of my first sketches

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.163
and color drawings of the
different Disney characters,

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:26.994
and it's been a really good experience.

00:04:26.994 --> 00:04:29.661
(relaxed music)

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:33.670
- [Interviewer] So,
what's this building here?

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:35.580
- So, this is Kresge Auditorium.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:40.050
It's the main concert and
event space on campus,

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.770
and this is where most
of the music concerts,

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.860
and recitals, and events happen.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.650
- [Interviewer] Okay, and so what does

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.620
the music scene look like at MIT?

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.120
- So, there's a common
misconception around MIT,

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.583
that it's all engineering and just science

00:04:54.583 --> 00:04:55.416
and math.
- [Interviewer] Numbers.

00:04:55.416 --> 00:04:57.130
- And numbers all the time.
- [Interviewer] Numbers.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.300
- But that's totally not true.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.900
There's a very vibrant art scene.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.800
There's a very vibrant music scene.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:05.180
I've mainly been involved
in the music scene here.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.070
There's a jazz ensemble.
There's choruses.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.300
There's orchestras, symphony orchestras,

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:14.023
1 ensemble, African drumming groups,

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.220
pretty much anything
that you could imagine.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.360
And the administration's
really, really supportive

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.520
of students that wanna
get involved in the arts

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.000
because, you know, everybody here

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.300
is studying some form of
engineering or science,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:30.300
so the art and music groups, you know,

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:31.670
have a really, really nice camaraderie,

00:05:31.670 --> 00:05:34.473
because everyone is doing it
purely because of passion.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.977
(relaxed music)

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:54.540
- [Interviewer] So, what
do you major in at MIT?

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.740
- So, I'm majoring in
mechanical engineering

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.470
with a flexible
concentration that allows me

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:01.700
to focus on something else,

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.410
and that concentration is
entertainment technology.

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:06.840
- Is that a flexible
concentration that you built,

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:08.160
or was that sort of pre-existing?

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:10.150
- So, there are some pre-existing ones

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:13.090
like energy, transportation, robotics,

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:16.400
but I decided to create my own flexible,

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.500
custom concentration in the
entertainment technology.

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:19.940
- [Interviewer] And what
did that process look like,

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.010
building that concentration?

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.190
- So, it's surprisingly simple.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:26.350
MIT does have a lot of
support for, you know,

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:30.390
students kinda making their
own versions of their majors

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.100
and Course 2, mechanical engineering,

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:35.690
has an option that you
can submit a proposal

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:38.450
with a list of classes that are relevant,

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:40.310
and you, you know, write some essays

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.500
about why it's important to you,

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.360
why each class contributes to this,

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:45.760
and you just make the proposal,

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.200
and you talk to your advisor

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:49.460
and, hopefully, you can get approved.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.630
- Great, great, what do you think makes

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:55.130
the MIT Engineering Department
different or special

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:57.140
as compared to other
schools, from your knowledge?

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:58.680
- So, I think there's been a big push

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.730
in the past couple decades, you know,

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:03.790
to increase STEM
education, and engineering,

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:05.860
but the thing about MIT
is that they've been

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:07.390
doing it long, long before

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:10.040
it was ever cool to be building things.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.139
- That's not the concern,

00:07:11.139 --> 00:07:11.972
the cool factor.
- Right.

00:07:11.972 --> 00:07:13.570
And the professors here, you know,

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:15.680
they're the people that
invented the internet,

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.450
invented all of these technologies

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:20.270
that, you know, are so
ubiquitous to us now.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:24.150
So, just the amount of community knowledge

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:27.950
is just unfathomable, and
you get to learn from these

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:31.883
true leaders in every
single technological field.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.270
So, you never have to
worry that, you know,

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:36.410
you're learning the right thing,

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:39.140
or you're thinking about things correctly,

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.760
because they're just so
good at what they do.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:43.527
They're just like, "'Kay,
this is what you gotta know.

00:07:43.527 --> 00:07:44.627
"You're gonna be in this situation.

00:07:44.627 --> 00:07:46.097
"You're gonna have to
think about it this way.

00:07:46.097 --> 00:07:49.250
"So, you need to learn this,
and that, and et cetera."

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:53.230
So, it's just really, really
helpful to be in class,

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:55.320
and to be able to just
completely pay attention

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.810
and be like, "Okay, I know
what I'm supposed to know."

00:07:57.810 --> 00:07:58.750
And then just go do it.

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:00.050
- - [Interviewer] Yeah, yeah.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:01.750
- And I think there's a huge focus

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.150
at MIT on practical learning,

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:05.000
and I think that that's something

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.050
that a lot of other schools
and a lot of other communities

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:09.810
are trying to ramp up,
but the idea of, you know,

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.760
learning through doing, and
MIT's motto is "Mind and Hand."

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:14.650
So, you both, you know, study the theory,

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.650
but then you have really
intense lab classes,

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:19.760
and MIT really actually
gives you tangible skills

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:22.330
that you can use to build
things, to make things,

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:27.330
to design things that, you
know, are hard to get sometimes.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.720
- Do you have a good example
of a class where you had a cool

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:33.490
learning-by-doing experience?
- Yeah.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:36.610
So, the mechanical
engineering capstone class

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:39.720
is called 2.009, it's the
product engineering process,

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.690
and it's a class, truly, like no other.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.410
So, there's 8 teams of students.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.750
Each gets a budget of about $7,000,

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:48.920
and over the course of the semester,

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:50.680
the 20 people on your team have to

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:54.050
come up with a product, design it,

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:57.607
build it, and then pitch it
by the end of the semester.

00:08:57.607 --> 00:08:58.440
- - [Interviewer] Do you do every stage?

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.273
- Every single stage.

00:08:59.273 --> 00:09:00.480
You're basically like a little startup,

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:03.830
and you go from, you know,
a blank sheet of paper

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:06.360
to being on a stage in Kresge, you know,

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:07.950
pitching your product to the world.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:11.300
And, you, know, you have
space in lab to do it,

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.360
you have professors, you
have technical instructors

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.300
to help you, communication instructors.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:19.240
But just the experience of
being given the opportunity

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.980
to be in a leadership
position at such a young age

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:25.270
when you're just coming off of all of

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.530
your rigorous technical education, like,

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.340
you actually learn a lot
of the other problems

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:32.090
that go into making anything,

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:33.700
because it's not just the
equations and everything.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:34.750
It's, you know, how do
you deal with people?

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.900
How do you design for a human being?

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:39.970
How do you, you know, design
something to be manufactured?

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:42.830
And having the opportunity in a safe,

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:44.690
you know, constructive
learning environment,

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:46.930
to do that when the stakes aren't huge

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:48.501
is incredibly valuable.

00:09:48.501 --> 00:09:51.168
(relaxed music)

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:04.940
- Before I let you go and do

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:05.897
all the work that you gotta do.

00:10:05.897 --> 00:10:06.910
- Yeah.
- Do you have any

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:09.150
parting wisdom for high school students,

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.690
any, like, mantras?

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:13.820
- Yeah, so I think
something that I live by

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:16.480
is the idea that it's
okay to be in the middle,

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.210
and to not, you know,
live in the extreme world,

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:21.307
and not pigeonhole
yourself in one discipline.

00:10:21.307 --> 00:10:24.550
And it's okay to both
like art and engineering,

00:10:24.550 --> 00:10:26.840
or both wanna do music and do

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.080
something completely different,

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:29.780
and it's okay to have different interests,

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:31.210
and it's really important, you know,

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:33.850
to show that if you're
applying to college,

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:36.253
or just life in general, generally,

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.980
'cause those parts of you that, you know,

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.640
you don't always service
through your school major

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:44.820
or through your research,
they're still parts of you.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.734
So, they need to be celebrated.

00:10:46.734 --> 00:10:49.401
(relaxed music)

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.200
So, if you like this video and wanna learn

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:56.231
more about colleges,
don't forget to subscribe.

00:10:56.231 --> 00:10:58.898
(relaxed music)

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.770
Alright, so if you like this video

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:12.770
and you wanna learn more--
(Interviewer laughs loudly)

00:11:13.007 --> 00:11:14.207
- [Interviewer] Do you
need to come back through?

00:11:14.207 --> 00:11:15.709
- No, that's okay, I got
my finger on the button.

